{"kind":"request","text":"claude: make the repository path in the workroom UI a link to its remote, when there is one. Hugh asked for this today. Sequenced after your current repair lanes; nothing here is urgent.\n\nWHAT HE ASKED FOR, in his words: where we display the repo path, if there's a remote url, it should hyperlink to that remote url. He notes that navigating straight to the remote is fine, because a forge like GitHub redirects https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq.git to the right page by itself, so we do not need to translate the URL into a web URL.\n\nWHERE IT IS TODAY, checked rather than guessed. TopBar.tsx lines 50 to 52 renders workroom.repo inside a plain span with a title attribute and no link. The value reaches it from /v0/worktrees, whose payload is worktreesResponse in internal/service/ui.go line 129, built from app.LocalRepo in internal/app/app.go line 174. Neither the response nor LocalRepo carries a remote at all, and neither does the client type in ui/src/lib/api.ts line 277. So this is not only a UI change: the remote has to be read and carried through app and service before TopBar can render it.\n\nTHE SECURITY PART IS THE REAL WORK HERE, and I want it decided deliberately rather than discovered later. A git remote URL is configuration, not a value this codebase generates, and turning it into an href puts it into the DOM.\n\nThree things I would want a reviewer to insist on. First, credentials: a remote may be https://user:token@host/org/repo.git, and rendering that as a link puts the token in the DOM, in hover text, in copy-link, and in the Referer header on navigation. Strip userinfo, or decline to link when it is present, and say which you chose. Second, scheme: ssh remotes such as git@github.com:org/repo.git and ssh:// forms are not navigable, and an href is only safe for http and https. Allowlist those two schemes and fall back to today's plain text for everything else. An allowlist rather than a denylist, because a remote URL is attacker-influenceable for anyone who clones from a hostile source, and a javascript: or data: scheme reaching an href is stored cross-site scripting on a page that holds actor custody. Third, if the link opens in a new tab, rel=\"noopener noreferrer\", where noreferrer also keeps the local resident URL out of the forge's logs.\n\nThe existing comment on LocalRepo argues that the served checkout's absolute path is safe to show because gs serve refuses any non-loopback listen address, so the reader is already on this host. That reasoning still holds and bounds the blast radius, but it does not cover the scheme or credential points, because those are about what the page does with a value rather than who can see it.\n\nTWO SMALLER DECISIONS I AM LEAVING TO YOU. Which remote, when a repository has several or none; origin is the obvious convention but say so rather than letting the code imply it. And whether the path stays visible next to the link or is replaced by it, which is a judgement about what a reader is actually looking for in that corner of the bar.\n\nI have not pre-decided the shape and I do not think this needs to be large. It should be a few fields and one anchor element.","body":{"conditions":"The repository path shown in the workroom top bar links to the repository's remote URL when one exists and is safely linkable, and renders exactly as it does today when it is not. Carry the remote from git through app.LocalRepo and the /v0/worktrees response to the client type in ui/src/lib/api.ts and on to TopBar.tsx. Only http and https URLs become an href, by allowlist and not by denylist; every other scheme, ssh and scp-style git@host:org/repo forms included, renders as today's plain text. Userinfo is removed from the rendered URL and from the href, or the link is declined when userinfo is present; state which you chose and why. If the link opens a new tab it carries rel=\"noopener noreferrer\". State which remote is used when a repository has several, and behave sanely with none. Regression tests written from the desired behaviour, not transcribed from current output, covering at least: an https remote links; an ssh or scp-style remote does not and falls back to plain text; a javascript: or data: scheme never reaches an href; a remote carrying userinfo does not leak it into the DOM; and no remote renders exactly as today. Prove mutation-sensitivity by replacing the scheme allowlist with a permissive check and watching the named cross-site-scripting assertion fail, then reverting. Rebuild the embedded bundle with make ui and keep make ui-check green in the same head, since the resident serves the committed embed rather than ui/src. Exact clean pushed head, per-path artifacts at every changed stable path including the two UI files which currently have none, gofmt, build, vet, npm test and go test ./... -count=1 green, independent review recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge. Sequenced after your decision-records and receipt-checkpoint repair lanes; not urgent.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}